    ,
      "why": "Fully pipelined gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax. To hit >=1 MSPS we need Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially met; the real cost driver is area (N+2 stages of datapath). Accuracy <= 2^-10 requires enough iterations: CORDIC error is roughly 2^-(n_iter+1) in the worst case, so n_iter >= 10-11 is the floor. data_width drives both area and output resolution; 12-18 covers the accuracy floor (W=12 gives Q1.10 output, matching the 2^-10 spec exactly) up to a modest oversize. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small to control area since accuracy is already met by n_iter. Rounding is cheap and can shave error, so include both."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >=1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area (luts+ffs) as the primary objective. Only pipelined and pipelined_m families are available to me, and both produce 1 result/cycle, so throughput is simply Fmax. Since Fmax on Artix-7 for a CORDIC datapath is typically 100-300 MHz, the throughput constraint is easily satisfied by either family. The binding constraints are therefore accuracy and area. For accuracy: CORDIC residual error after N iterations is bounded by ~2^-(N+1), so N >= 10 is needed to guarantee max_abs_err <= 2^-10. I set n_iter range to 10-16 to explore the accuracy/area trade-off. For area: data_width W determines datapath width and thus LUT/FF count linearly. W=12 gives Q1.10 output which exactly matches the 2^-10 error spec, so W=12 is the minimum viable width. I explore W=12-18 to capture the full trade-off curve. angle_guard and frac_guard add bits to internal paths and increase area; since accuracy is governed primarily by n_iter, I keep these ranges tight (angle_guard -1..2, frac_guard 0..2) to avoid wasting budget on area-inflating configurations that don't improve the objective. I allocate all budget to the pipelined family in this first round because it has the simplest structure (one register per stage, N+2 latency) and its area scales predictably with N and W, making it the clearest baseline for the Pareto front. pipelined_m can be explored in a later round if the front suggests that reducing register count (at the cost of higher latency) yields a better area point. The selection rule is min luts_plus_ffs, so the front will be dominated by low-N, low-W configurations that still meet the accuracy constraint; the hypervolume objective on accuracy_bits will pull the search toward slightly higher n_iter to capture the accuracy dimension."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The spec is feasible and the Pareto front is well-mapped within the constraints. The minimum area design (pipelined, data_width=15, n_iter=13) achieves 1332 LUTs+FFs, which is below the hypervolume reference of 1500, and meets the throughput constraint (273 MSPS >= 1 MSPS) and accuracy constraint (10.2 bits >= 10 bits). The front extends up to 13.1 bits accuracy, providing a good trade-off curve. Since this is the final round, we stop and select the design with minimum luts_plus_ffs (1332) as per the selection rule."
